<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#ReclamationMovement> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SacredEntity> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#evidentiarySources> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The documentary, scholarly, or oral sources used as the basis for reconstruction. Range: iroko:DocumentaryEvidence."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#return-other> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Return type not covered by existing concepts."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#type-re-africanization> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Efforts to restore African elements to Afro-Atlantic traditions that have been transformed by colonialism, syncretism, or assimilation. Exemplified by the Candomblé re-Africanization movement in Bahia and the movement to remove Catholic saint imagery from Lucumí/Ocha practice."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#hostCommunity> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#House> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SlavePaperPeriodScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#hasTopConcept> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#period-1808-1820> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#rel-influence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#method-community-consensus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Reconstruction through formal community process: elders and practitioners deliberating together to establish or restore practice."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#ReconstructionMethodScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Métodos de reconstrução"@pt .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#returnOutcome> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The documented result of this return: mutual recognition, initiation, rejection, or ongoing relationship. Free-text."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SlavePaper> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A colonial legal document — sale record, ship manifest, customs certification, baptismal register entry, judicial filing, manumission instrument, or freedom petition — that constituted, maintained, challenged, or forged an ownership claim over an enslaved person in the Atlantic world. The class name follows the TiSP (Trafficking in Slave Paper) project terminology, which foregrounds the documentary nature of the legal mechanism: slave paper was not merely a record of completed transactions but an active legal instrument through which status, ownership, and identity were continuously made and contested. Slave paper required perpetual renewal through notarial, customs, ecclesiastical, or judicial systems; the gap or absence in that chain was itself a legal event. SlavePaper instances may serve as iroko:evidentiarySources for iroko:ReconstructedPractice and populate iroko:evidenceBase on iroko:HeritageRelationship where the enslaved person's cultural or religious identity can be traced. Contributor roles for producers of slave paper (notary, scribe, commissioner) are modeled in iroko-authority.ttl via iroko:ContributorRoleScheme."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#afroAtlanticTradition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko-sankofa> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#ReturnTypeScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Trans-Afro-Atlantic Return Types"@en .
