<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#evidencesHeritage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Links a SlavePaper instance to the HeritageRelationship it evidences — typically because the document records the name, origin, or cultural identity of an enslaved person whose tradition can be traced. This is the ontological connection between the forced-departure documentation and the reclamation arc: the slave record that proves a transmission chain is the evidentiary link between forced removal and the heritage claim."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#movementContestation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "contestation notes"@en .
_:nf755d2f3941d4074a3cf2e3e90f6d57ab8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#sourceTradition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The African tradition identified as the reclamation's reference point. Range: iroko:TraditionScheme concept."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#keyFigures> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "key figures"@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#paperFunction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#groundedIn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The heritage relationship assertion that grounds this return event or reclamation movement. A TransAfroAtlanticReturn or ReclamationMovement is typically predicated on a claimed relationship between an Afro-Atlantic tradition and an African source tradition; this property links the event or movement to that formal assertion. The heritage claim may be widely attested, lineage-specific, or contested — the property records the claim without adjudicating it."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#RecognitionStatusScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Status de reconhecimento"@pt .
