<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#paper-type-ship-manifest> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A customs or port document listing enslaved persons as cargo aboard a vessel. Required for transatlantic and coastal trade; used to collect duties and establish initial entry into the colonial documentary record."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#returnType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko-sankofa> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Domain module. Vocabulary for the full Afro-Atlantic arc: the forced-departure side, documenting the colonial legal instruments that constituted and contested ownership over enslaved persons (iroko:SlavePaper and the SlavePaper type, function, and period schemes); and the return side, documenting revitalization movements, Trans-Afro-Atlantic returns, reconstructed traditions, and heritage relationships. The module name follows the Akan sankofa principle — to go back and fetch what was taken — a structure that encompasses both what was taken (documented in slave paper) and the subsequent return to claim it. Models semantic relationships between Afro-Atlantic practice and African source traditions without adjudicating authenticity. All heritage relationship claims are modeled as iroko:RelationshipAssertion to reflect their contested and negotiated character. The classes and properties in this module are descriptive instruments — they document what movements call themselves and what they claim to do. The use of terms such as 'return', 'diaspora', and 'reclamation' reflects the self-description of the movements documented here, not a normative position of the Iroko Framework on questions of African identity or belonging. Use with iroko-authority to document who may authorize heritage relationship claims and recognition, and for contributor roles (notary, scribe, commissioner) associated with slave paper production; iroko-narrative for movement histories, oral transmission chains, and contested origin accounts."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#paper-type-manumission-instrument> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Carta de libertad"@es .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#paper-type-freedom-petition> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A legal petition filed by or on behalf of an enslaved person seeking recognition of free status, disputing ownership, or claiming coartación rights. Freedom petitions are the primary site of enslaved legal agency within the colonial documentary apparatus."@en .
