<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#groundedIn> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#minimumAccessLevel> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#access-public-unrestricted> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#paper-function-freedom-basis> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Document constitutes or supports the legal basis for an enslaved person's free status — manumission instruments, freedom petitions, and successful court rulings."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#period-pre-1808> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Pre-1808"@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#type-other> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#MovementTypeScheme> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#geographicLocus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The city, country, or region where this movement, return event, or reconstructed practice was primarily based or most active. Language-tagged string. Examples: 'New York City (1920s–1940s)'@en for Garveyite reclamation; 'Bahia, Brazil'@pt for the Candomblé re-Africanization effort; 'Port-au-Prince, Haiti'@fr for a Vodou governance movement. For machine-readable geographic linking, use iroko:hasJurisdiction (from iroko-authority.ttl) instead of or in addition to this property."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#movementOutcome> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#ReclamationMovement> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#type-repatriation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Physical return of Afro-Atlantic people to African source communities, creating new hybrid communities and exchanges. Examples: Brazilian returnee (aguda/tabom) communities in West Africa who brought Afro-Atlantic Candomblé elements back to Benin and Nigeria."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#HeritageRelationship> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Heritage Relationship"@en .
